# "Radiance" in the Dark
Tamil title: இருட்டில் “பிரகாசம்”

"I insist that the government ban every one of Annadurai's publications and that they must not be permitted to enter this country."

You may ask, "Who is this tsar?" Yet it is an ordinary man who has issued this pronouncement. Had such words been spoken by a despot swollen with the arrogance of power and a passion for repression, there might be some reason to ponder them. But the man making this demand is no such person. He is a devout Saivite who prays every day, "Mother and Father, gather me at your feet." He is reported to have spoken in this fashion at the Conference of Saivite Workers held in Point Pedro, Ceylon. Many comrades angered by his speech have been writing to us. Ordinarily we "forgive" such "true devotees" and leave matters there. But because of the trouble our comrades are causing us, I am obliged to introduce him to you.

The name of the true devotee who thus opened his sacred mouth is Sivapragasam. He is said to be a learned Tamil scholar. His name is Pragasam, "Radiance"; indeed, Siva-pragasam, "Siva's Radiance"! Comrades attended eagerly expecting the ideas of such a devotee to cast the light of knowledge. But "Radiance" led them into darkness. That is why they write to us.

I wish to tell the comrades whose letters are emotional, angry and condemnatory one thing: do not cultivate needless hostility over the fury of these "true devotees." Show sympathy for their having fallen into this condition! Instead of praying to the God whom they worship daily, "Friend in adversity! Protector of the helpless! Destroy this wrongdoer, for he weakens religious faith," they now kneel before the rulers. That itself is a victory for us.

For if they have come to fall at the feet of temporal rulers without trusting Lord Siva, who possesses "a trident, a whirling sword, hosts of spirits who run at his command, the eye upon his brow, and the power to destroy by mere thought," the meaning concealed within it is that they themselves have no faith in God. Had they faith, would they court the government?

If the "Light of Wisdom that fills all space" regarded our work as improper, would he simply leave us alone? By now he could have summoned Yama and commanded, "Cast the noose around every Self-Respecter and bring them here," and destroyed us all!

Therefore we ask our comrades not to become angry over the true devotee's "insistence." Reading in *Dinakaran* the passages reporting that the true devotee Pragasam spoke emphatically for banning Annadurai's books, we feel the deepest pity for the whole company of true devotees.

He is reported to have said: "I wish particularly to mention the publications of a South Indian writer named Annadurai... He has been providing certain things that enchant and intoxicate the youth of the country... The Vedas and Agamas composed by ancient and eminent sages have been condemned and reviled by him."

This mournful song from a sacred servant who praises the consort of blue-hued Sakti, eternally true and pervading all without exception, delights us! Our writings, it seems, "enchant and intoxicate." Our ideas, it seems, pierce through the power of the "Vedas and Agamas composed by ancient and eminent sages." So says the true devotee! What a victory this is.

If the very ordinary ideas of people like us can pierce arrangements made by eminent sages whom true devotees celebrate as having mastered the eight great supernatural powers and meeting God every day, what does that mean? Either those arrangements must be full of holes and fractures, or "God himself" must detest them as useless.

The true devotee himself will say that God is formless, a light pervading all, present in you, in me, in the world and throughout the universe. In whatever manner such a God exists as his "soul," must he not likewise perform his sacred dance within "our" souls?

The true devotee himself will preach, as you may have heard, that "the movement of the world is God's dance." Are we too not persons moved by that God? God, whom he worships, must therefore have entered upon this divine sport of using us to curb the insolence of these true devotees. Otherwise, would he grant only us the power to write words that "enchant the young"? Would he spread through the people, by our agency, a disposition to reject the Vedas and Agamas?

Had God grown angry and asked, "How dare you find fault with us?" he would not have left us alone all these years. He would have carried us away long ago. Apparently, then, our continued existence gives him pleasure! To Ramana, who spent every day worshipping at Tiruvannamalai, he gave a tumour; he did not give it to us. Do not forget this truth.

You may ask why these true devotees alone should become angry when God himself has not become angry with us. When people invite someone to deliver a religious discourse and offer cow's milk, a beautiful garland and an honorarium to each speaker, to whom do they make those offerings? To God? No, no: to these true devotees. That is why they become angry!

It is not for God that abbots and true devotees rage against us; it is for themselves, from fear that their ever-comfortable sacred bodies might wither.

If "radiance" arose everywhere, darkness would cease to exist. Then we could no longer conduct our "night occupation" in society. It is from that sorrow that these true devotees and holy lords have set out to speak in this fashion.

Who are they to foster devotion to God or make arrangements for it? If they desire liberation while living, should they not remain where they are, chanting "Hara Hara Mahadeva!" and journeying towards the supreme abode? Instead, should they occupy themselves with futile investigations of this illusory world, asking what one man does and how another lives?

If they possessed genuine "true devotion" to God, would they insist and speak in all these ways?

We therefore inform our comrades that they need not devote thought to this sacred company, which speaks in this fashion for its own sake.

A snake is protected only while it remains inside its anthill. If it comes out and hisses, a rational human being will not let it go! Instead of quietly accepting that their time has run only this far, they have introduced themselves...

---NOTES---
The title puns on the speaker's name, Sivapragasam: பிரகாசம் means radiance or illumination, which the article places “in darkness.” The source alternates சிவப்பிகாசம் and சிவப்பிரகாசம்; the historically and linguistically coherent form Sivapragasam is used in English while the Tamil remains verbatim. “Point Pedro” is the standard English name for பருத்தித்துறை in northern Ceylon. The final surviving scan ends mid-sentence after “தங்களை அறிமுகப்”; the translation preserves that incomplete boundary.
